  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 313 AM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nFor the rest of the week, the bulk of the forecast remains centered \non subtle differences in temperatures from day to day, although \nconfidence remains high that we will remain at least slightly above \naverage through the end of the week. As mentioned in previous \nforecasts, the upper flow pattern will be characterized by a broad, \nstagnant ridge across the western CONUS, with northwesterly to quasi-\nzonal flow overhead for at least the first half of the week. At some \npoint this upper ridge is expected to drift eastward into the \nMississippi River Valley and eventually the eastern CONUS, \nalthough confidence in this evolution decreases considerably \nover the weekend. \n\nWhile surface high pressure and a dry airmass will continue to be \nthe driver of sensible weather through the end of the workweek, \nthere are some subtle developments that may cause some minor changes \nfrom day to day. The first of these features is a weak cold front \nthat is expected to approach the area sometime late \nWednesday/Thursday, and ensemble guidance has steadily increased its \ninfluence on forecast temperatures over the past couple of days. \nIt's still only a matter of a few degrees, but this front may slow \nour warming trend Wednesday-Friday, especially north of I-70. Not \nonly this, but we also note some modest moisture pooling showing up \nalong this front, and a smattering of light QPF and/or cloud cover \nalong this boundary...most notably Friday as it lifts back north as \na warm front. Still, moisture levels remain very low, and we have \nvery little confidence that this will yield any meaningful \nrainfall. Instead, we will maintain the going forecast for \ntemperatures roughly 5-10 degrees above average through the work\nweek, with warmest readings across the Ozarks and southwest IL.\n\n\nAs mentioned previously, over the weekend is when the synoptic \npattern is set to change more substantially, although exactly how \nquickly this occurs will determine the day-to-day specifics. The \npotential exists for temperatures to warm slightly as the upper \nridge moves overhead and southerly low level flow resumes, and there \nare also hints that this could bring some increasing humidity back \nto the north...and potentially even some precipitation. Temperature \nspreads remain high late in the forecast period, and there is little \nconsistency among members when it comes to precipitation timing or \nlocations, but there is at least some hope that this pattern change \nwill at least open the door for it.",
